1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,540
You staged two files, and then you notice that one of them does not belong in this commit.

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:07,384
Maybe it is half finished, or you added it by accident.

3
00:00:07,604 --> 00:00:09,071
You do not have to undo the edit.

4
00:00:09,291 --> 00:00:14,799
In this video you will unstage a file in GitTree, so it leaves the next commit and keeps every change.

5
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:17,263
Unstaging does not touch your work.

6
00:00:17,483 --> 00:00:19,169
Your edits live in the working folder.

7
00:00:19,389 --> 00:00:22,961
The staging area is only a list of what the next commit will hold.

8
00:00:23,181 --> 00:00:28,130
Unstaging takes a file off that list, and the file on disk stays exactly as you left it.

9
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,087
Here is the demo project.

10
00:00:30,307 --> 00:00:34,228
In the lesson on staging a file, you learned how a file gets into this list.

11
00:00:34,448 --> 00:00:39,577
Two files are staged, the readme and the tags helper, so both would go into the next commit.

12
00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:41,403
We want to commit only the readme.

13
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:45,090
Point at the file you want to remove, here the tags helper.

14
00:00:45,310 --> 00:00:49,600
A minus button appears at the end of its row, named Unstage File. Click it.

15
00:00:50,367 --> 00:00:53,180
The tags helper is back under Unstaged Files.

16
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:57,880
Only the readme is left under Staged Files, so only the readme goes into the next commit.

17
00:00:58,633 --> 00:00:59,701
Nothing was lost.

18
00:00:59,921 --> 00:01:04,111
Open the file, and the function you added is still there, waiting in your working folder.

19
00:01:04,867 --> 00:01:05,954
There is a faster way.

20
00:01:06,174 --> 00:01:08,259
Click a staged file, then press U.

21
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:13,428
It runs the same command, Unstage, and it works whenever the file list has focus.

22
00:01:13,648 --> 00:01:16,741
The readme moved back too, and now nothing is staged.

23
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:22,299
In plain Git, the command is git restore --staged, followed by the path.

24
00:01:22,519 --> 00:01:25,432
Run git status --short before and after.

25
00:01:25,652 --> 00:01:29,613
The first column is the staging area, and the second is the working folder.

26
00:01:29,833 --> 00:01:33,715
After the restore, the tags helper has its letter in the second column only.

27
00:01:33,935 --> 00:01:39,781
To unstage everything at once, git reset -q does it, and GitTree has an Unstage All button.

28
00:01:40,533 --> 00:01:45,243
To recap: unstaging takes a file out of the next commit, and never touches your edit.

29
00:01:45,463 --> 00:01:48,077
Use the Unstage File button, or press U.

30
00:01:48,297 --> 00:01:50,741
Download GitTree free at gittree.app.

31
00:01:50,961 --> 00:01:53,805
In the next video, you will write a message and make the commit.

